          body: 'Before a cell divides, it must copy all of its DNA so that each daughter cell gets a full set. In the last topic you saw that the bases are tucked inside the helix. A copying enzyme cannot read bases it cannot reach, so the double helix has to be opened. Copying is **semiconservative**: each new double helix contains one old strand and one newly built strand. The old strand acts as a **template**, a pattern whose bases dictate which bases are added to the new strand. This was shown in 1958 when bacteria grown on heavy nitrogen (¹⁵N) were moved to light nitrogen (¹⁴N): after one round of copying, every DNA molecule was a hybrid of one heavy and one light strand.',
